{
  "id": "jingyun_dagu",
  "name": "Jingyun dagu (Beijing-style drum song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Sung narrative (quyi) shaped in Tianjin and Beijing in the late Qing and early Republican years from the Hebei wooden-clapper drum song, adopting Beijing pronunciation; listed as national intangible cultural heritage in 2006. A standing singer beats a small flat drum on a stand with a single stick and holds a pair of clappers in the other hand, accompanied by the large sanxian and the four-string sihu, sometimes with pipa (the acoustic_membrane_drum card stands in for the shugu drum and the paiban card for the clappers).",
  "recipe": "classic East Asian mid-2000s jingyun dagu, ballad Poetry. solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. membrane drum pulse acoustic membrane drum, paiban, sanxian, sihu, pipa. chinese traditional teahouse. large-diaphragm condenser, German V72 tube pre, German tube console, broadcast loudness EQ. tape 15ips hall reverb.",
